# Day 9 – SQL String Functions

Today I learned about **String Functions in SQL**.

These functions are useful when working with text data, like names, emails, usernames, and other text columns.

## 1. UPPER()

Converts text into uppercase.

```sql
SELECT UPPER(name) AS name_upper
FROM employees;
```

Example:

```text
Rahul → RAHUL
```

## 2. LOWER()

Converts text into lowercase.

```sql
SELECT LOWER(name) AS name_lower
FROM employees;
```

Example:

```text
Rahul → rahul
```

## 3. LENGTH()

Returns the number of characters in a string.

```sql
SELECT name, LENGTH(name) AS name_length
FROM employees;
```

Example:

```text
Rahul → 5
```

## 4. CONCAT()

Used to join two or more strings together.

```sql
SELECT CONCAT(first_name, ' ', last_name) AS full_name
FROM employees;
```

Example:

```text
Rahul + Kumar → Rahul Kumar
```

## 5. SUBSTRING()

Used to extract part of a string.

```sql
SELECT SUBSTRING(name, 1, 3) AS short_name
FROM employees;
```

If the name is `Rahul`, the result would be:

```text
Rah
```

The exact syntax can vary slightly between SQL databases.

## 6. TRIM()

Removes unnecessary spaces from the beginning and end of a string.

```sql
SELECT TRIM(name)
FROM employees;
```

This is useful when data contains accidental spaces.

Example:

```text
"  Rahul  " → "Rahul"
```

## 7. REPLACE()

Replaces part of a string with another value.

```sql
SELECT REPLACE(email, 'gmail.com', 'outlook.com')
FROM employees;
```

Example:

```text
rahul@gmail.com → rahul@outlook.com
```

## Using String Functions Together

We can also combine functions.

```sql
SELECT UPPER(TRIM(name)) AS cleaned_name
FROM employees;
```

This first removes extra spaces and then converts the name to uppercase.

## Why String Functions Matter

Real-world databases aren't always perfectly clean.

We might have:

* Extra spaces
* Different uppercase/lowercase formats
* Long text that needs to be shortened
* Names stored in separate columns
* Text that needs to be replaced or cleaned

String functions help handle these situations directly inside SQL.

